{"id":"neet-2026r-q105","pn":105,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Glycolysis products","q":"How many molecules of pyruvic acid are produced at the end of glycolysis from 206 molecules of glucose?","o":["309","103","412","206"],"c":2,"s":"One glucose molecule gives two molecules of pyruvic acid at the end of glycolysis.\nSo 206 glucose molecules give 206 × 2 = 412 molecules of pyruvic acid."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q128","pn":128,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Respiratory quotient","q":"2(C₅₁H₉₈O₆) + 145O₂ → 102CO₂ + 98H₂O + energy\nThe Respiratory Quotient (RQ) of a biomolecule used for respiration, as per the above equation would be:","o":["Less than 0.5","Between 0.5 and 0.95","Between 1.25 and 2","1.0"],"c":1,"s":"RQ = (Volume of CO₂ evolved)/(Volume of O₂ consumed).\nFrom the equation, CO₂ evolved = 102 and O₂ consumed = 145.\nRQ = 102/145 = 0.7.\nThis value lies between 0.5 and 0.95.\nNote: The paper prints C₆ in this formula; C₅₁H₉₈O₆ (a triglyceride) is meant."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q135","pn":135,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Site of respiratory steps","q":"Match List-I with List-II :\nList-I (Process) — List-II (Location)\nA. Glycolysis — I. Inner mitochondrial membrane\nB. ETS — II. Mitochondrial matrix\nC. Accumulation of protons — III. Cytoplasm\nD. Krebs' cycle — IV. Intermembrane space","o":["A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"c":1,"s":"Glycolysis takes place in the cytoplasm in all living organisms.\nThe electron transport system is located in the inner mitochondrial membrane.\nProtons accumulate in the intermembrane space.\nKrebs' cycle takes place in the mitochondrial matrix."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q091","pn":91,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Electron transport chain complexes","q":"The complex II of mitochondrial electron transport chain is also known as","o":["Cytochrome bc₁","Succinate dehydrogenase","Cytochrome c oxidase","NADH dehydrogenase"],"c":1,"s":"Complex II of the mitochondrial electron transport chain is succinate dehydrogenase.\nCytochrome c oxidase is complex IV.\nNADH dehydrogenase is complex I.\nCytochrome bc₁ is complex III."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q143","pn":143,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Krebs cycle","q":"Identify the step in tricarboxylic acid cycle, which does not involve oxidation of substrate.","o":["Succinyl-CoA → Succinic acid","Isocitrate → α-ketoglutaric acid","Malic acid → Oxaloacetic acid","Succinic acid → Malic acid"],"c":0,"s":"Oxidation is loss of electrons (often as hydrogen) from a molecule, so its oxidation state rises.\nThe electrons go to an acceptor, which is reduced.\nConversion of succinyl-CoA to succinic acid does not involve oxidation of the substrate."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q144","pn":144,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Site of respiration steps","q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList I — List II\nA. Citric acid cycle — I. Cytoplasm\nB. Glycolysis — II. Mitochondrial matrix\nC. Electron transport system — III. Intermembrane space of mitochondria\nD. Proton gradient — IV. Inner mitochondrial membrane.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below.","o":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"c":3,"s":"The citric acid cycle occurs in the mitochondrial matrix.\nGlycolysis occurs in the cytosol in most organisms.\nThe electron transport system is in the inner mitochondrial membrane.\nThe proton gradient is built up in the intermembrane space.\nSo A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q134","pn":134,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Glycolysis ATP use","q":"Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :\nAssertion A : ATP is used at two steps in glycolysis.\nReason R : First ATP is used in converting glucose into glucose-6-phosphate and second ATP is used in conversion of fructose-6-phosphate into fructose-1-6-diphosphate.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A is false but R is true.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false."],"c":1,"s":"ATP is used in two steps of glycolysis.\nStep 1: glucose → glucose-6-phosphate.\nStep 2: fructose-6-phosphate → fructose-1,6-bisphosphate.\nATP is used to phosphorylate the substrates, so R correctly explains A."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q148","pn":148,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Stages of respiration","q":"Match List I with List II :\nList I: A. Oxidative decarboxylation; B. Glycolysis; C. Oxidative phosphorylation; D. Tricarboxylic acid cycle\nList II: I. Citrate synthase; II. Pyruvate dehydrogenase; III. Electron transport system; IV. EMP pathway\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A – II, B – IV, C – III, D – I","A – III, B – IV, C – II, D – I","A – II, B – IV, C – I, D – III","A – III, B – I, C – II, D – IV"],"c":0,"s":"Pyruvate undergoes oxidative decarboxylation in the mitochondrial matrix by pyruvate dehydrogenase: A-II.\nGlycolysis is the EMP pathway: B-IV.\nThe electron transport system makes the proton gradient for phosphorylation, so it is oxidative phosphorylation: C-III.\nThe TCA cycle starts when citrate synthase joins acetyl group with OAA: D-I."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q106","pn":106,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"TCA cycle intermediates","q":"The 5-C compound formed during TCA cycle is:","o":["Fumaric acid","α-ketoglutaric acid","Oxalo succinic acid","Succinic acid"],"c":1,"s":"α-ketoglutaric acid has 5 carbons.\nFumaric acid has 4 carbons.\nOxalosuccinic acid has 6 carbons.\nSuccinic acid has 4 carbons."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q109","pn":109,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Fermentation energy","q":"What amount of energy is released from glucose during lactic acid fermentation ?","o":["More than 18%","About 10%","Less than 7%","Approximately 15%"],"c":2,"s":"Less than 7% of the energy in glucose is released in lactic acid fermentation.\nNot all of it is trapped as ATP bonds."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q127","pn":127,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Glycolysis ATP yield","q":"What is the net gain of ATP when each molecule of glucose is converted to two molecules of pyruvic acid?","o":["Six","Two","Eight","Four"],"c":1,"s":"Glycolysis makes 4 ATP from one glucose.\n2 ATP are used up in the process.\nNet gain = 4 - 2 = 2 ATP."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q131","pn":131,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Krebs cycle decarboxylation","q":"The number of time(s) decarboxylation of isocitrate occurs during single TCA cycle is:","o":["Four","One","Two","Three"],"c":1,"s":"The TCA cycle has two decarboxylation steps.\nIn step 4 isocitrate loses H atoms and CO₂ and becomes the 5-carbon α-ketoglutarate.\nIn step 5 α-ketoglutarate reacts with coenzyme A and gives CO₂ and the 4-carbon succinyl-CoA.\nThe question asks only about isocitrate, so the answer is one."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q132","pn":132,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Amphibolic pathway and related terms","q":"Match List - I with List - II:\nList - I: (a) Porins (b) leg haemoglobin (c) H⁺ accumulation (d) Respiration\nList - II: (i) Pink coloured nodules (ii) Lumen of thylakoid (iii) Amphibolic pathway (iv) Huge pores in outer membrane of mitochondria\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"c":2,"s":"Porins are proteins that make large pores in the outer membrane of mitochondria, plastids and bacteria.\nLeghaemoglobin is the pink pigment of legume root nodules and mops up O₂ for N₂ fixation.\nH⁺ collects in the thylakoid lumen.\nRespiration is amphibolic because it has both breakdown and build-up roles."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q144","pn":144,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Electron transport chain","q":"Which of the following statements of incorrect?","o":["Oxidation-reducation reactions produce proton gradient in respiration","During aerobic respiration, role of oxygen is limited to the terminal stage","In ETC (Electron Transport Chain), one molecule of NADH + H⁺ gives rise to 2 ATP molecules, and one FADH₂ gives rise to 3 ATP molecules","ATP is synthesized through complex V"],"c":2,"s":"The NCERT values are 3 ATP per NADH and 2 ATP per FADH₂, so statement 3 has them swapped and is incorrect.\nRedox reactions build the proton gradient, O₂ is the final electron acceptor, and ATP synthase is complex V, so those statements are correct."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q036","pn":36,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Respiration in Plants","st":"Krebs cycle","q":"The number of substrate level phosphorylations in one turn of citric acid cycle is :","o":["One","Two","Three","Zero"],"c":0,"s":"In one turn of the citric acid cycle, succinyl-CoA to succinate forms one GTP/ATP by substrate level phosphorylation.\nSo the number is one."}
